# Chapter 10: Conversion.  The chapter is kept in three sources that this
# file reads: the guide and the ways in and out (this file), the ways in and
# out in detail (convert1), and the rest, with the messages and the examples
# (convert2).
.doc Strata Reference: Conversion
.chapter 10
.h1 Conversion [convert]
.ix Conversion
.ix Conversion utilities
.ix Import and export, overview
.ix Chapter 10
.ix Formats of other programs
Strata reads and writes plain text, makes PDF and TeX files of a document,
takes pictures in four formats, and reads old documents (the
documents of the earlier format).  This chapter says exactly what that means,
how to use it, what happens at the edges, and how to bring in a file that
Strata cannot read itself.

There is nothing in this chapter that is hidden behind a key of its own.
The conversions are the commands Import and Export of the File menu of the
Editor, which the chapter on files describes entry by entry ({n:file-import}
and {n:file-export}) and the Printer, which is the way out to a PDF.  Those entries are not described again here.  This chapter adds
what they leave open: how the conversions compare, what survives each of them,
how to bring in a file that Strata cannot read itself, and how to get a
document out in the form that another program takes.

The chapter is arranged for looking up.  A table, {x:convert-guide}, answers
the question "I have this, I want that".  {x:convert-have} lists every way in
which data enters and leaves Strata.  The facts about text that comes in and
goes out, with the recipes that depend on them, are in {x:convert-import} and
{x:convert-export}; the Printer as a way out is {x:convert-printer} and the
pictures are {x:convert-pictures}.  What is to be done with equations of an old
document is in {x:convert-equations}, how old documents are read is in
{x:convert-release}, and the word list of the spell check is in
{x:convert-dict}.  The messages are in {x:convert-messages} and the worked
examples in {x:convert-examples}.

.h2 What do I do for ...  [convert-guide]
.ix Conversion guide
.ix Look-up table, conversion
.ix How to convert
The table is a way into the chapter.  The right-hand column names the section
that has the steps.
.table 24 36
I have | What to do
A plain text file | Import (File, Import); {x:convert-import}.
A text file in UTF-8 | Import with the Character Set UTF-8;
 {x:convert-import-utf8}.
A table in a spreadsheet | Save it as text with tabs; Import with Preserve
 Line Endings; set the ruler; {x:convert-recipe-table}.
A letter or an article from another word processor | Save it as plain text
 there; Import with Ignore Line Endings; {x:convert-recipe-prose}.
An outline in a text file | Import it and make the sections;
 {x:convert-recipe-outline}.
A picture in another format | Convert it to PNG or JPEG;
 {x:convert-pictures}.
An old document | Retrieve it; {x:convert-release}.
An equation of an old document | Rewrite it in TeX; {x:convert-equations}.
A document that is to be mailed or edited elsewhere | Export; {x:convert-export}.
A document that is to be printed or sent | The Printer: PDF;
 {x:convert-printer}.
A list of words for the spell check | A text file; {x:convert-dict}.
.end

.h2 The ways in and out [convert-have]
.ix Ways in and out
.ix Import, ways in
.ix Export, ways out
.ix Conversions, table
Every conversion of Strata is one of the rows below.  The first column says
what comes in or goes out, the second how it is done, and the third where it
is described.
.table 18 20 20
What | How | Described in
Plain text in | File, Import in the | {x:file-import}
 Editor |
Plain text out, as a screen | File, Export, Export a | {x:file-export}
 image | Screen Image
Plain text out, as data | File, Export, Export Data | {x:file-export}
 | Only
A part of a document | Save, Extract Current | {x:file-save-options}
 | Section
A whole document, a part of | Retrieve | {x:file-retrieve}
 one, or settings |
A document to PDF | Print Final or Draft | {x:convert-printer}
A document to TeX | Export a LaTeX File | {x:convert-printer}
Pictures in | The Picture marker | {x:convert-pictures}
Equations in | The Equation marker, TeX | {x:convert-equations}
 math |
Text in or out by the | The terminal's own paste | {x:appchar-type}
 terminal |
.end
All of the rows but the last work on the text of one document at a time.  The File
menu has two commands for text, Import and
Export.  They read and write text, in the character sets of the Character Set
field ({n:appchar-pages}).
.h3 What stays when a document goes out as text [convert-have-lost]
.ix Loss of information on export
.ix Attributes, lost in text export
.ix Fonts, lost in text export
Text does not carry everything that a document has.  This table says what is
lost in each way out, so that the choice can be made knowingly.
.table 28 15 15
What the document has | Screen image | Data only
Characters | kept | kept
Bold, italic, underscore | lost | lost
Fonts, sizes, spacing | lost | lost
The layout of columns and tables | kept | lost
Tabs | by the ruler | as tabs
A blank line between blocks | one | none
Section numbers | kept | lost
The title page | lost | lost
Pictures, equations | a name | lost
Keycaps and other values | their text | their text
Notes | a mark | lost
.end
The title page of the document, which is the text above the first level of a
structured document, is not written by Export.  The marker of a note is its mark in the display mode (by default [NOTE]) in both;
its text is in neither.

